\chapter{Independent type, memory, and advisory analysis}
\label{ch:89}
\index{Independent type, memory, and advisory analysis}
\index{LibStone!Chisel}
\index{LibStone!Nerotypical}

Nerotypical can independently validate a concrete structural HIR subset through its prepared solver SDK. Chisel checks lowered resource control flow for admitted ownership and loan profiles. Each requires a compatible optional build and can reject internal representations it cannot model. Wetstone's bounded advisory analysis and Syren reporting address a different job: informing the user and, in supported cases, proposing separately checked repairs.

\section{Interpret tool outcomes}
Use a valid scalar sample and a violation designed for each analysis. Include an unsupported feature so the test distinguishes failed validation, unavailable solver configuration, and a skipped advisory. A reported proof applies to the modeled obligations and input. A compiler-valid repair can still change user intent. Compare source differences and run a behavior oracle when adopting one. Keep the source checker, canonical verifier, optional analysis and native boundary in the report so readers know which component established each result.

\section{Ask what each checker must reconstruct}
Start with a checked function returning a pair of an integer and a
Boolean. In an internal test, alter the stored result annotation so that
the fields appear in reverse order, without changing the expression that
constructs the pair. A verifier that just reads back the annotation has
no independent evidence of a mistake. Nerotypical reconstructs a product
of arity two and constrains its components from the expressions as well
as the result signature. Matching arity is insufficient: the integer
and Boolean constructors cannot unify in the swapped positions. This
is a type obligation even when the program allocates no resources.

Now consider a JSON view obtained from a local root, used in a nested
block. Correctly naming the view type is insufficient to justify its
use. The Chisel model records owner creation and the shared loan,
checks uses in the control-flow graph, and ends the loan before dropping
the root on a normal scope exit. By contrast, an ordinary On1x lexical
borrow of a scalar is outside that adapter's modeled events. Successful
frontend admission and refusal of the optional check are compatible
outcomes, not opposing verdicts about a dangling pointer.

Finally distinguish a repair suggestion from either of these proofs.
The repair command checks candidate edits through an entry-file overlay;
only a unique compiler-valid output may be created at a fresh path.
Multiple different valid edits are ambiguous, and Wetstone advisory
text is not an edit. Even a uniquely compiling correction must still
be compared with the function the author meant to write. A changed output
for one representative call can refute the proposed repair even after every
compiler check on the edited program passed.

